    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## Exercise 6: Critical Reflection\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Reflect on the use of image-based representations, such as matrix-structured recurrence plots, for encoding time-series data.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Discussion Points\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Address the following aspects in your analysis:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. **Strengths of recurrence plot representations**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - How do recurrence plots capture temporal dynamics that raw time series might not reveal?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - What patterns (e.g., periodicity, regime changes) become more visible in the RP domain?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. **Limitations of recurrence plot representations**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - What information might be lost during the transformation?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - How sensitive are recurrence plots to noise, time series length, and parameter choices (e.g., ε)?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. **Data storage implications**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - Compare the storage requirements of raw time series vs. recurrence plot images.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - How does upsampling (35 → 140 time steps) affect storage?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. **Computational efficiency**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - What is the computational cost of generating recurrence plots?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - How does the feature dimensionality of flattened RP images compare to raw time series?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"5. **Feature extraction and machine learning**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - Why might image-based representations benefit from convolutional neural networks (CNNs)?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"   - How does K-NN performance compare across the two representations and why?\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# TODO: Write your reflections here as comments or markdown\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# You can also add quantitative analyses to support your discussion\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Total ROI pixels:\\\", total_train_px + total_test_px)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Example: Compare storage requirements\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ts_size = (total_train_px + total_test_px) * 37 * 3 * 4 # size in bytes for raw time series\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Time series data's size:\\\", ts_size, \\\"bytes =\\\", ts_size/1024/1024/1024, \\\"gigabytes\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"rp_size = (total_train_px + total_test_px) * 140 * 140 * 3 # size in bytes for recurrence plot images\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Recurrence plot data's size:\\\", rp_size, \\\"bytes =\\\", rp_size/1024/1024/1024, \\\"gigabytes\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Storage ratio (RP / TS): {rp_size / ts_size:.1f}x\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Example: Compare feature dimensionality\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ts_dim = 37 * 3   # number of features in raw time series\\n\",\n",
    "    \"rp_dim = 140 * 140 * 3   # number of features in flattened RP image\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Feature dimensionality — TS: {ts_dim}, RP: {rp_dim}\\\", \\\"\\\\nDimensionality ratio:\\\", f\\\"{rp_dim/ts_dim:.1f}x\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Your Reflection\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Analysis\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. Strengths of recurrence plot representations\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A recurrence plot does not show the value of a pixel, but when it returns to its original state. This gives you a similarity map which shows temporal patterns more easily. Because of this you can see periodicity (= repeated diagonal bands), regime changes (= shift in texture/block structure), chaotic and random behavior (= short diagonal lines or isolated points), laminar/stable states (= vertical or horizontal lines) and recurring motives (=repeating geometric patterns). This gives extra information in temporal information like this. Time series only plot values against time and therefore only show what happens at adjacent time points, not when the pixels value returned to a similar state and how those returns occurred over time.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. Limitations of recurrence plot representations\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A recurrence plot loses the information of the actual raw trend. This means that amplitudes, trends, and temporal ordering are mostly lost, because of the pairwise comparisons. They are also sensitive to noise, which can leave meaningful structures as fragmented patterns or spurious recurrences. The recurrence plots are relatively sensitive to noise, as we have not included a recurrence threshold (this is a parameter that also parameter that influences the sensitivity). For K-NN classification, this would be counterproductive, since the information on how close neighbors are is lost with the binary classification. A longer time series creates a more meaningful recurrence plot; there are more values to compare, which can show patterns that are otherwise not visible.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. Data storage implications\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The shape of the time series is 37*960*1280*3, datatype float32. The total storage of the recurrence plots after upsampling is 140*140*960*1280*3, which is a significant difference compared to no upsampling (37*37*960*1280*3) and especially compared to the time series. Two different types of recurrence plots are made in this notebook; one version uses datatype uint8 and is used for showing some RGB recurrence plots. The other version, used for training the K-NN classifier, uses datatype float32, which causes these recurrence plots to take up 4 times less storage than the other version.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. Computational efficiency\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A drawback of recurrence plots is their high computational cost. Generating an RP requires calculating pairwise distances between all timepoints. This leads to approximately O(N²) complexity in both time and memory. Additionally, flattening an RP image often creates a much higher-dimensional feature space than the original time series. This increase can raise storage needs and make classification more difficult.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"To tackle this issue, we can apply dimensionality reduction before classification. In our case, we also looked at reducing the image dimensionality by focusing on the most informative color channels. The blue channel usually provides less insight into plant phenotypic changes. In contrast, the red and especially the green channels capture more biologically relevant variation. By removing the less informative channels, we can lower computational costs while keeping the most important patterns.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"K-NN suffers from the curse of high-dimensionality. This means that in a high dimensional data space, the datapoints become sparse and the distances become less meaningful. With so many features and not a lot of datapoints, K-NN struggles to correctly identify the pixels. In order to lessen this effect, we tried removing the blue channel. K-NN gave the highest accuracy for only the green channel, a combination of the red and green channels gave the second best accuracy, followed by all three channels combined.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"We also applied Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to the recurrence plot feature vectors as an additional way to reduce the dimensionality of the data. Applying PCA on the feature vectors extracted from the recurrence plots can remove redundant or low-information features, potentially lessening the ‘curse of dimensionality’ and improving the performance of the KNN classifier. Applying PCA before KNN halved the runtime of the KNN. However, despite the reduction in dimensionality and runtime, it did not lead to an improvement in classification accuracy.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"5. Feature extraction and machine learning\\n\",\n",
    "    \"CNN-based representations should generally outperform the extraction of raw features. This is because they actually learn spatial patterns and do not only look at individual pixels independently. The network captures relationships between local pixels (gives edges, textures, shapes, etc.) with the use of convolution; pooling gives a degree of translation and scale invariance. This gives a hierarchical extraction of features that makes CNN's a very robust choice compared to raw feature approaches, specifically in variations and region-of-interest size. This is practically useful in data with a lot of shifting in the target area.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This is contrasting compared to the K-NN performance, which depends strongly on the quality of the feature space. When this is actually applied to raw or flattened image data, Euclidean distance even loses meaning in high dimensions. This, “curse of dimensionality”, can make points that are not really similar appear close together anyways. This results in weaker classification performance. However, when K-NN uses better representations, such as CNN-extracted embeddings or engineered features that capture structure, it can actually perform significantly better. In these cases, distances reflect real similarity. Overall, CNNs learn better representations directly from data, while K-NN relies heavily on having a well-structured feature space from the start.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This results in a worse performance of K-NN in raw pixel representations, and K-NN will improve when it is applied to CNN-based embeddings, where the distance becomes meaningful again.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Discussion\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Position of masks\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The distance from the center of the photo and area of the ROI seems to impact the quality of the predictions for those species. Because the photographs were captured using a hemispherical lens, canopy regions located near the edges of the image are visually compressed and appear more densely packed, making species identification more difficult than in the center of the image, where the canopy appears sparser. This was especially evident for species that had their training mask in the middle and their test mask far on the side, or vice versa. This pattern can be observed by the low accuracy in the confusion matrix for M. rubiginosa and P. ramiflora. The photos were taken in a period where leaf senesce takes place and it is harder for the model to capture this senesce when the canopy looks so densely packed. Identifying species that are pushed to the side in the picture will therefore not result in the expected accuracy. The distortion in the picture adds another layer of difficulty on top of the classification problem.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Size of masks\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Furthermore, next to the position of the mask, the size of the mask also seems to influence the predictive value of the model. The training mask for P. ramiflora was less than half the size of the testing mask (1283 vs 3001 pixels). P. ramiflora had the smallest training set out of all and it was the most difficult species for the model to identify accurately. It is difficult to determine whether the limited training data or the positional differences between the training and test masks had a greater impact on the model, as both likely contributed to the poor results. But, in the end the goal is to develop a model that can reliably identify plant species regardless of their position within the image, which this current model is still struggling with. We switched the training and test set around to see to what extent the choice of mask influences the accuracy of the classification. Accuracy improved to some extent compared to the initial setup, ranging between 0.2 and 0.3 However, manually looking for the best train-test mask combination might give a better result within this context, but will not improve the strength of the model when applied to different photo series.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Species-based classification\\n\",\n",
    "    \"In the confusion matrix, we observed that P. ramiflora was frequently misclassified as P. torta. This may be explained by the fact that both species belong to the same genus and are therefore likely to share phenotypic characteristics. Currently, our model treats all misclassifications the same and does not distinguish between confusing P. ramiflora with P. torta and confusing it with a more evolutionary distant species. From a biological perspective, the latter type of error may have a more serious implication than the former when researching the vegetation in this area of Brazil.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"To address this, we could explore grouping species at the plant family (or genus) level rather than at the species level. This would transform the classifier from species classification to family classification and would likely increase the model’s accuracy. However, this would result in groups instead of single species, and whether this trade-off is acceptable depends on what the researchers/ stakeholders want.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Importance of Time-Series Length\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Finally, the length of the image time series likely influenced the classification results. Recurrence plots are designed to capture temporal dynamics and patterns of change, but our dataset only covered a period of 37 days at the end of summer. This timeframe may have been too short to capture substantial phenotypic changes in the vegetation. For example, leaf senescence could occur later in the season.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"As a result, the absolute values in the time series may have contributed more to the classification performance than the temporal patterns captured by the recurrence plots. Nevertheless, recurrence plots could prove to be  valuable when applied to datasets covering a longer portion of the year, in which more pronounced phenotypic changes are seen.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
